WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.320
Speaker 1: Bill Barr, Donald Trump's former&nbsp;
attorney general, continues to attack his&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:04.320 --> 00:00:09.660
former boss. You may remember that last week&nbsp;
Bill Barr commented on the indictment having to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:09.660 --> 00:00:14.760
do with the alleged mishandling of classified&nbsp;
documents when he went on Fox News Sunday and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:14.760 --> 00:00:20.400
said that if even half of the indictment is&nbsp;
true, then Trump is toast. He also rebuked&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:20.400 --> 00:00:24.540
Trump's legal team's claims that the matter&nbsp;
should fall under the Presidential Records Act&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:24.540 --> 00:00:29.640
rather than the Espionage Act in full. Bill&nbsp;
Barr said that if even half of it is true,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:29.640 --> 00:00:35.100
then he is toast. I mean, it's a pretty it's&nbsp;
a very detailed indictment and it's very,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:35.100 --> 00:00:40.260
very damning. And these were big statements, of&nbsp;
course, because of who they were coming from,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:40.260 --> 00:00:46.260
the top prosecutor in the Trump administration&nbsp;
for a time, Bill Barr. Trump then responded to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:46.260 --> 00:00:52.200
Bill Barr statements by calling him a gutless&nbsp;
pig. Well, fast forward to this weekend. This&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:52.200 --> 00:00:58.560
past weekend, when Bill Barr was on Face the&nbsp;
Nation on CBS, and Barr is actually stepping&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:58.560 --> 00:01:04.380
up the criticism. He's not backing down. He's now&nbsp;
calling Trump a consummate narcissist who will put&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:04.380 --> 00:01:10.860
his own interests, his own ego above everything&nbsp;
else. He also called Trump a defiant nine year old&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:10.860 --> 00:01:17.220
kid and a fundamentally flawed person who engages&nbsp;
in reckless conduct. So there's a couple of clips&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:17.220 --> 00:01:22.620
I want to play for you from this interview. The&nbsp;
first has to do with the legal arguments. Barr&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:22.620 --> 00:01:26.340
decimates the idea that Trump had the right&nbsp;
to take the records under the Presidential&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:26.340 --> 00:01:31.260
Records Act and could simply unilaterally just&nbsp;
classify them without going through a process,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:31.260 --> 00:01:36.180
telepathically or otherwise. Bill Barr says&nbsp;
that he had absolutely no right to do that,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:36.180 --> 00:01:40.980
and he had no right to possess the documents.
Speaker 3: The legal theory by which he gets&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:40.980 --> 00:01:46.740
to take battle plans and sensitive national&nbsp;
security information as his personal papers&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:46.740 --> 00:01:51.960
is absurd. It's just as wacky as the legal&nbsp;
doctrine they came up with. You know, having&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:51.960 --> 00:01:57.660
the vice president unilaterally determine who won&nbsp;
the election. The whole purpose of the statute,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:57.660 --> 00:02:03.660
the Presidential Records Act, is was to stop&nbsp;
presidents from taking official documents out of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:03.660 --> 00:02:08.160
the White House. It was passed after Watergate.&nbsp;
That's the whole purpose of it. And therefore,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:08.160 --> 00:02:12.480
it restricted what a president can take. It&nbsp;
says it's purely private. That had nothing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:12.480 --> 00:02:21.060
to do with the deliberations of government policy.&nbsp;
Obviously, these documents are not purely private.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:21.060 --> 00:02:25.080
It's obvious. And they're not even now arguing&nbsp;
that it's purely private. What they're saying&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:25.080 --> 00:02:30.120
is the president just has sweeping discretion to&nbsp;
say they are, even though they squarely don't fall&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:30.120 --> 00:02:34.320
within the definition. It's an absurd argument.
Speaker 1: So Barr says it. The Presidential&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:34.320 --> 00:02:38.580
Records Act was meant to restrict the rights&nbsp;
of presidents, not to grant them immunity or&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:38.580 --> 00:02:42.420
give them more power. And it's not even&nbsp;
relevant to this case because this has&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:42.420 --> 00:02:47.640
to do with national secrets, not just Trump's&nbsp;
daily diary, anything innocuous. So it's not&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:47.640 --> 00:02:51.480
something that would fall under the Presidential&nbsp;
Records Act. This falls under the Espionage Act.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:51.480 --> 00:02:55.920
And then we get into the fun part. This is when&nbsp;
Bill Barr talks about the possibility of Trump&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:55.920 --> 00:03:01.380
becoming the nominee and becoming president&nbsp;
again and the impact that that would have&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:01.380 --> 00:03:05.220
on the country. Let's take a look at this.
Speaker 3: Trump, as you know, has many good&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:05.220 --> 00:03:12.360
qualities, and he accomplished some good things.&nbsp;
But the fact of the matter is he is a consummate&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:12.360 --> 00:03:21.240
narcissist and he constantly engages in reckless&nbsp;
conduct that that puts his political followers at&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:21.240 --> 00:03:26.280
risk and the conservative and Republican agenda&nbsp;
at risk when he put the country at risk if he was&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:26.280 --> 00:03:30.540
in the White House again, he will always put&nbsp;
his own interests and gratifying his own ego&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:30.540 --> 00:03:34.920
ahead of everything else, including the country's&nbsp;
interests. There's no question about it. This is a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:34.920 --> 00:03:39.135
perfect example of that. He's like, you know, he's&nbsp;
like a nine year old. A defiant nine year old kid&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:39.135 --> 00:03:43.440
is always pushing the glass toward the edge of the&nbsp;
table, defying his parents to stop him from doing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:43.440 --> 00:03:49.740
it. It's a means of self-assertion and exerting&nbsp;
his dominance over other people. And he's a very&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:49.740 --> 00:03:55.500
petty individual who will always put his interests&nbsp;
ahead of the country's, his personal gratification&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:55.500 --> 00:04:00.720
of his, you know, of his ego. But our country,&nbsp;
our country can't, you know, can't be a therapy&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:00.720 --> 00:04:06.060
session for, you know, a troubled man like this.
Speaker 1: Blistering words there from Bill Barr.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:06.060 --> 00:04:11.880
But let's put it all into perspective. Barr was&nbsp;
also critical of Donald Trump when it came to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:11.880 --> 00:04:17.340
January 6th and the allegations of voter fraud in&nbsp;
the 2020 election. He famously would not go along&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:17.340 --> 00:04:23.340
with the plan to try to overturn the election&nbsp;
results. And he resigned a few weeks before&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:23.340 --> 00:04:29.580
the end of the Trump administration. But then. He&nbsp;
was asked if he'd vote for Trump over a Democrat,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:29.580 --> 00:04:35.700
and he actually said yes, that the threat of&nbsp;
Donald Trump is not greater than the threat&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:35.700 --> 00:04:42.060
of a progressive being put into power. So Barr&nbsp;
believes that Trump is like a nine year old child.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:42.060 --> 00:04:46.080
He believes that he's a cosmic narcissist.&nbsp;
He believes that he's someone who puts his&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:46.080 --> 00:04:51.840
own interests in front of the country, someone&nbsp;
who is wrong on the law, wrong on the morals,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:51.840 --> 00:04:57.900
someone who is presumably guilty of crimes. And&nbsp;
yet Barr thinks that that guy is a better vote&nbsp;&nbsp;

